.TH MONOSIGNER 2
.SH NAME
monosigner: secret, pub, pubs, addpub, save, pkof, keypath \- the monoca signer key
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "monosigner.m";
sgn := load Monosigner Monosigner->PATH;

secret: fn(path, keyfile: string): (array of byte, string);
pub:    fn(path: string): (array of byte, string);
pubs:   fn(path: string): (list of array of byte, string);
addpub: fn(path: string, pk: array of byte): string;
save:   fn(path, keyfile: string, sk: array of byte): string;
pkof:   fn(sk: array of byte): array of byte;
keypath: fn(explicit: string): string;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Monosigner
owns the file formats around the monoca signer key, so that the CA
commands share one implementation and verifiers never open the secret.
.PP
The secret lives in
.IR path
(conventionally
.BR /keydb/mono-signer ).
A plaintext file is one line:
.IP
.EX
sk \fI128 hex chars\fP
.EE
.IP
An encrypted file records how to derive its key:
.IP
.EX
signer 2
kdf argon2id
salt \fI16 bytes, hex\fP
blocks 1024
passes 3
nonce \fI24 bytes, hex\fP
tag \fI16 bytes, hex\fP
ct \fI64 bytes, hex\fP
.EE
.IP
The ciphertext is the 64-byte EdDSA secret key under XChaCha20-Poly1305
.RB ( aeadlock ,
.IR mono (2)),
and the record key is Argon2id over the key file's contents and the
salt.  The parameters are stored with the record, so they can be raised
later without breaking old files.
.PP
.B secret
returns the 64-byte secret key, decrypting when needed.  The
key-encryption key comes from
.I keyfile
when it is not empty, otherwise from
.B /n/host/keydb/mono.key
when that exists.  The file holds either 64 hex characters (a raw
32-byte key) or a passphrase.  A plaintext signer is accepted as it is.
Derived keys and the passphrase are wiped before returning.
.PP
.B pub
returns the first trusted public key, and
.B pubs
returns all of them: it reads
.IB path .pub
and expects one
.IP
.EX
pk \fI64 hex chars\fP
.EE
.IP
line per key, falling back to deriving the public half from the secret
when the public file is missing.  A rotation adds the new key with
.B addpub
before the old one is retired, so certificates and epochs signed by
either key keep verifying during the change.
.PP
.B save
writes
.I sk
to
.I path
(encrypted when a key is available) and publishes its public half in
.IB path .pub
with mode 644.  The secret file is written with mode 600.
.B pkof
returns bytes 32 to 64 of a 64-byte secret key;
.B keypath
resolves the key file from an explicit name or the default.
.SH FILES
.TF /n/host/keydb/mono.key
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer
the secret, plaintext or encrypted; mode 600
.TP
.B /keydb/mono-signer.pub
one or more trusted public keys; mode 644
.TP
.B /n/host/keydb/mono.key
default key-encryption key, supplied by the host
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/monosigner.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mono (2),
.IR monoca (8),
.IR monoauth (8),
.IR certify (8)
.SH BUGS
A passphrase is only as strong as Argon2id with 1024 blocks and three
passes, about a megabyte and a few milliseconds; the raw-key form is
preferred for unattended machines.
.PP
The public file has no revocation date: keys are added and removed by
editing the file.  Removing a key that signed the current epoch makes
that epoch unverifiable, so retire an old key only after a new epoch
has been published and distributed.
